WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.368
Foundation Two motion gets easier the moment you stop treating every journey number as if it answers the same question.

2
00:00:05.368 --> 00:00:10.737
This lesson really separates three ideas.

3
00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:16.105
Distance asks how much ground was covered in total.

4
00:00:16.105 --> 00:00:21.473
Displacement asks where the traveller finished relative to the start, and it keeps direction.

5
00:00:21.473 --> 00:00:26.842
Average speed asks how much total distance was covered in each second of the whole trip.

6
00:00:26.842 --> 00:00:32.210
If those three questions are blended together, the worked example feels slippery.

7
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:37.578
If they stay separated, the numbers become much easier to trust.

8
00:00:37.578 --> 00:00:42.947
Keep this takeaway in view: Ask the right question first: whole route, start-to-finish change, or distance-per-time for the whole journey.

9
00:00:42.947 --> 00:00:48.315
Distance means full path.

10
00:00:48.315 --> 00:00:53.683
Add every part of the route, including return sections and detours.

11
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:59.052
Displacement means net change.

12
00:00:59.052 --> 00:01:04.420
Compare finish with start and keep the direction of that overall change.

13
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:10.812
The cleanest picture here is the map picture.

14
00:01:10.812 --> 00:01:17.204
Distance behaves like tracing the actual route with your finger.

15
00:01:17.204 --> 00:01:23.596
Every turn, every extra stretch, and every return leg adds to that total.

16
00:01:23.596 --> 00:01:29.988
Displacement behaves like drawing one arrow from the starting point straight to the finishing point.

17
00:01:29.988 --> 00:01:36.380
That is why a journey can have a large distance but a small displacement.

18
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:42.772
It is also why a round trip can build distance while leaving the final displacement at zero.

19
00:01:42.772 --> 00:01:49.164
The route and the arrow belong to the same journey, but they are not the same quantity.

20
00:01:49.164 --> 00:01:55.556
Keep this takeaway in view: A detour can increase distance dramatically while leaving displacement unchanged if the finish stays in the same place.

21
00:01:55.556 --> 00:02:01.948
Distance: Uses every stage of the route, even if the path loops or wanders.

22
00:02:01.948 --> 00:02:08.340
Displacement: Only depends on the start and finish positions, plus the direction between them.

23
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:14.146
A very strong memory hook for this lesson is odometer versus map arrow.

24
00:02:14.146 --> 00:02:19.953
The odometer stands for distance because it keeps counting the whole route that was actually travelled.

25
00:02:19.953 --> 00:02:25.759
If the driver takes a wrong turn, the odometer still increases.

26
00:02:25.759 --> 00:02:31.565
The map arrow stands for displacement because it only joins the start and finish.

27
00:02:31.565 --> 00:02:37.372
If two travellers end at the same place but one took a long scenic detour, their odometer readings differ but their map arrows can match.

28
00:02:37.372 --> 00:02:43.178
That analogy is powerful because it gives each quantity its own job.

29
00:02:43.178 --> 00:02:48.985
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy only works if you keep the jobs separate: counting route is not the same as comparing positions.

30
00:02:48.985 --> 00:02:54.791
Odometer means distance.

31
00:02:54.791 --> 00:03:00.597
Counts every extra segment that was actually travelled.

32
00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:06.404
Map arrow means displacement.

33
00:03:06.404 --> 00:03:12.210
Ignores the wandering details and keeps only the overall change.

34
00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:18.084
Average speed sits beside distance and displacement in this lesson because it is also a whole-journey idea.

35
00:03:18.084 --> 00:03:23.958
The formula is average speed equals total distance divided by total time.

36
00:03:23.958 --> 00:03:29.832
The word total matters twice.

37
00:03:29.832 --> 00:03:35.707
Use the full distance of the whole trip, and use the full time of the whole trip.

38
00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:41.581
Students often grab one fast stage or one obvious number and divide too soon.

39
00:03:41.581 --> 00:03:47.455
But average speed only becomes trustworthy when every second of the trip and every metre of route has been included first.

40
00:03:47.455 --> 00:03:53.329
Keep this takeaway in view: Average speed is a full-trip quantity, just like distance is a full-route quantity.

41
00:03:53.329 --> 00:03:59.203
Do use: Total path length and total time for the complete trip.

42
00:03:59.203 --> 00:04:05.078
Do not use: Only the fastest stage or an average of stage speeds unless the lesson specifically asks for that.

43
00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:10.952
average speed = total distance / total time.

44
00:04:10.952 --> 00:04:16.826
Collect the whole journey first.

45
00:04:16.826 --> 00:04:22.700
Then divide once.

46
00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:28.568
Watch how the method behaves on two short patterns.

47
00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:34.437
First, a traveller goes 16 metres east and then 16 metres west.

48
00:04:34.437 --> 00:04:40.305
Distance is 32 metres because the full path is added.

49
00:04:40.305 --> 00:04:46.173
Displacement is zero because the traveller finishes back at the start.

50
00:04:46.173 --> 00:04:52.042
Second, a scooter covers 150 metres in 30 seconds.

51
00:04:52.042 --> 00:04:57.910
Average speed is 150 divided by 30, so 5 metres per second.

52
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:03.778
These short patterns matter because they show that the route question and the speed question are different, even when both belong to the same journey topic.

53
00:05:03.778 --> 00:05:09.647
Keep this takeaway in view: Same topic, different questions: route length, net change, and whole-trip speed should never be merged into one answer.

54
00:05:09.647 --> 00:05:15.515
Out and back means 32 m.

55
00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:21.383
Distance keeps both stages, so it grows even though the traveller returns.

56
00:05:21.383 --> 00:05:27.252
Net change means 0 m.

57
00:05:27.252 --> 00:05:33.120
Displacement becomes zero because the finish matches the start.

58
00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:38.545
The main trap in this lesson is choosing an operation before choosing the meaning.

59
00:05:38.545 --> 00:05:43.969
If the question asks for distance, add the route segments.

60
00:05:43.969 --> 00:05:49.394
If it asks for displacement, compare finish with start and keep direction.

61
00:05:49.394 --> 00:05:54.818
If it asks for average speed, use the total distance and total time for the full journey.

62
00:05:54.818 --> 00:06:00.243
That is why subtracting every pair of route stages is wrong for distance, and averaging separate stage speeds is often wrong for overall average speed.

63
00:06:00.243 --> 00:06:05.667
The method must follow the meaning.

64
00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:11.092
Keep this takeaway in view: In motion questions, the quantity name is the instruction for the method.

65
00:06:11.092 --> 00:06:16.516
Wrong instinct: Subtract route segments just because one stage goes back the other way.

66
00:06:16.516 --> 00:06:21.941
Better habit: Say the quantity name out loud before you choose the arithmetic.

67
00:06:21.941 --> 00:06:27.365
Distance -> add every route segment.

68
00:06:27.365 --> 00:06:32.790
Displacement -> compare finish with start and keep direction.

69
00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:36.769
Pause for a fast self-check.

70
00:06:36.769 --> 00:06:40.748
If a traveller walks out and partly back, which answer uses every stage?

71
00:06:40.748 --> 00:06:44.728
Distance.

72
00:06:44.728 --> 00:06:48.707
Which answer only uses the starting and finishing positions?

73
00:06:48.707 --> 00:06:52.686
Displacement.

74
00:06:52.686 --> 00:06:56.665
If the whole trip was 240 metres in 40 seconds, what quantity comes from dividing those totals?

75
00:06:56.665 --> 00:07:00.645
Average speed, which would be 6 metres per second.

76
00:07:00.645 --> 00:07:04.624
Those three answers need to feel separate before you move on.

77
00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:08.603
Keep this takeaway in view: If you can name the quantity cleanly, the arithmetic usually becomes much more obvious.

78
00:07:08.603 --> 00:07:12.582
Whole route means distance.

79
00:07:12.582 --> 00:07:16.562
This is the add-every-segment quantity.

80
00:07:16.562 --> 00:07:20.541
Start to finish means displacement.

81
00:07:20.541 --> 00:07:24.520
This is the net-change-with-direction quantity.

82
00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:30.735
You are now ready for the worked example.

83
00:07:30.735 --> 00:07:36.951
The learner walks 10 metres east and then 4 metres west.

84
00:07:36.951 --> 00:07:43.166
Start with distance by adding the full path: 10 plus 4 gives 14 metres.

85
00:07:43.166 --> 00:07:49.382
Then switch to displacement and compare finish with start.

86
00:07:49.382 --> 00:07:55.597
After going 10 metres east and then 4 metres west, the learner is still 6 metres east of the start.

87
00:07:55.597 --> 00:08:01.812
So the two answers must be stated separately: distance equals 14 metres, displacement equals 6 metres east.

88
00:08:01.812 --> 00:08:08.028
If you keep that route-versus-arrow split, the example becomes a meaning exercise first and an arithmetic exercise second.

89
00:08:08.028 --> 00:08:14.243
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready to go: full route for distance, net change with direction for displacement.

90
00:08:14.243 --> 00:08:20.458
Step 1: Add every route segment for distance.

91
00:08:20.458 --> 00:08:26.674
Step 2: Compare finishing position with starting position for displacement.

92
00:08:26.674 --> 00:08:32.889
distance = 10 + 4 = 14 m.

93
00:08:32.889 --> 00:08:39.105
displacement = 10 m east - 4 m west = 6 m east.

94
00:08:39.105 --> 00:08:45.320
State both answers because they describe different ideas.
